LEVERAGE database migrations (Alembic)
=======================================

Adopted in the pre-AWS "DATABASE MIGRATION CONTROL" hardening phase, replacing
the previous mechanism: `models.Base.metadata.create_all()` plus three
hand-rolled `ALTER TABLE`/`CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS` blocks ("v005", "v006",
"v007") that ran on every single application startup. That mechanism is gone
from main.py's startup() as of this change -- schema is now versioned here.

Current revisions
------------------
  - `0e36d70f68ce` ("baseline_current_schema") -- generated by running
    `alembic revision --autogenerate` against a completely empty scratch
    PostgreSQL database (never against production), so `alembic upgrade head`
    from nothing reproduces the full, current, correct schema: all 25
    application tables, every foreign key and index declared in `models.py`
    (including `migration/models.py`'s `MigrationJob`), matching exactly what
    `models.py` describes today.
  - `a45e15c6abca` ("reconcile_production_drift"), head -- closes the gap a
    read-only production audit found between the baseline and the REAL live
    database (see "Schema drift" below). Every operation in it checks
    current state before acting, so it is a genuine no-op against a database
    that already has the baseline's full-correct shape (a fresh dev/CI
    database), and a real fix against production's drifted one -- the SAME
    migration file is correct for both paths. Fully tested against
    disposable databases (upgrade from empty, upgrade from a simulated
    drifted-production shape with real data, idempotency, downgrade, and a
    synthetic orphaned-row scenario proving it fails loudly rather than
    corrupting data) -- see tests/test_migration_drift_reconciliation.py.
    NOT yet applied to real production; requires explicit authorization.

Tables NOT managed by Alembic
-------------------------------
See `include_object` in `env.py`. Two are genuinely live/in-use and excluded
on purpose:
  - `candles_migration` -- a transient staging table owned entirely by the
    separate `migration/` package's own 1D-candle-migration tool.
  - `candles_aug2021_repair_bak` -- a manual one-off backup snapshot.

Five more were added to the exclusion list during the drift audit: the
`deprecated_intraday_candles_{5min,15min,30min,1h,1min}` and
`deprecated_intraday_ticks` tables were renamed live at some point (per the
DB-09 comments in models.py) and Postgres does not rename a table's indexes
along with it -- their indexes still carry pre-rename names (e.g.
`ix_intraday_candles_15min_id` instead of
`ix_deprecated_intraday_candles_15min_id`). Structurally the indexes are
identical to what models.py wants; only the names differ. Since these tables
are explicitly frozen/out of scope for modification, they're excluded
entirely rather than have Alembic propose a pointless DROP+CREATE pass
across them.

Onboarding the EXISTING production database
---------------------------------------------
This is now a TWO-STEP process (revised from the original single-stamp plan,
now that a real drift-fixing migration exists on top of the baseline):

    cd backend
    alembic stamp 0e36d70f68ce      # step 1: metadata-only, no DDL at all
    alembic upgrade head            # step 2: REAL DDL -- runs ONLY a45e15c6abca

Step 1 marks production as being at the baseline without touching anything
(`stamp` only ever writes one row to a new `alembic_version` table). Step 2
then genuinely executes `a45e15c6abca`'s DDL against production -- but by
that point Alembic only sees ONE pending migration (the baseline is already
stamped), so it applies exactly the reconciliation fixes below, nothing more.

Do NOT run `alembic stamp head` (stamping straight to the new head) for
production onboarding -- that would mark the drift-fix migration as "already
applied" without ever running its DDL, leaving production's real schema gap
completely unaddressed while claiming otherwise.

Neither step has been run against the real production database as part of
any phase so far; both require explicit authorization first.

A fresh database (a new AWS RDS instance, a local dev setup, CI) instead
just runs:

    cd backend
    alembic upgrade head             # applies both migrations from empty

Schema drift found by the pre-migration audit (now fixed by a45e15c6abca)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
A read-only audit against production (row counts, orphan/NULL checks, and
Alembic's own `compare_metadata` reflection tool) found:
  - 6 missing foreign keys: `positions.user_id`, `orders.position_id`,
    `orders.user_id`, `transactions.user_id`, `transactions.position_id`,
    `verification_tokens.user_id` -- confirmed 0 NULLs, 0 orphaned rows
    across all six before designing the fix.
  - `user_watchlist.user_id`'s FK exists but was missing `ON DELETE CASCADE`.
  - `user_chart_settings` had `UNIQUE(user_id)` in production vs. the
    model's `UNIQUE(user_id, ticker)` -- production could not store more
    than one ticker's settings per user. Table had 0 rows; zero-risk fix.
  - `orders.order_type` was `VARCHAR(10)` vs. the model's `VARCHAR(20)` --
    all existing values are 2 chars ('TP'/'SL'); pure widening.
  - `stock_metadata.is_active`/`is_premium` were NOT NULL in production
    (correctly, from the old v005 migration) but the model never declared
    that -- fixed in models.py itself (DRIFT-01 comment), 0 NULLs confirmed.

Deliberately NOT fixed (a judgment call for you, not applied by default):
  - `ix_candles_ticker` (and `ix_candles_id`): both are redundant with
    existing composite indexes that already lead with `ticker`
    (`uix_candle_key`, `ix_candle_ticker_tf_ts`). Every real query in this
    codebase that filters `candles` by ticker also filters by timeframe in
    the same call (verified by repo-wide grep) -- creating a dedicated
    single-column index would add real, permanent write cost to the busiest
    table in the system (17.6M+ rows, 5.7GB) for no measured read benefit.

Adding a new migration
-----------------------
    cd backend
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short_description"

Then READ the generated file before applying anything -- autogenerate is a
starting point, not a guarantee, especially for anything involving the
`candles` table, retention/timeframe logic, or the deprecated tables above.

Applying a migration
---------------------
    cd backend
    alembic upgrade head        # apply all pending migrations
    alembic current             # show the DB's current revision
    alembic history             # show the full migration chain

Never run `alembic upgrade`/`downgrade`/`stamp` against the production
database without explicit authorization, and never run a downgrade that
drops columns/tables against production at all -- downgrades in this
project are tested against disposable scratch databases only.

TLS configuration (POSTGRESQL TLS & NETWORK-TOPOLOGY SECURITY phase)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
`env.py` builds its own engine (separate from `database.py`'s, always has)
but now mirrors `database.py`'s TLS posture exactly, via the same two
environment variables:

  - `DB_SSLMODE` (default `prefer`, libpq's own default) -- passed straight
    through to `sslmode` on both engines. `prefer` is opportunistic: it
    silently falls back to a fully unencrypted connection if the server
    doesn't offer SSL, and never validates the server certificate even when
    SSL IS negotiated. Only `verify-ca`/`verify-full` do real certificate
    validation; `verify-full` additionally checks the hostname matches.
  - `DB_SSLROOTCERT` (default unset/omitted) -- path to a CA bundle file,
    only added to `connect_args` when non-empty. Required for
    `verify-ca`/`verify-full` to have anything to validate against.

Neither variable is set anywhere in this repo today (local `.env` files,
`docker-compose.yml`, or CI) -- this is a deliberate no-op by default,
verified by a live reconnect test against local Postgres with default
values unset. It was proven functional (including a real certificate
REJECTION with a mismatched CA) against a disposable self-signed-CA
`postgres:17-alpine` container using both the raw driver and this app's
actual `database.py` module -- see the PostgreSQL TLS & network-topology
security remediation phase's test coverage in
`tests/test_database_engine_config.py`
(`TestTlsSslConfigSource`/`TestTlsSslConfigLiveBehavior`).

To turn real certificate-validated encryption on in production, once (and
only once) the production Postgres/RDS hostname and CA bundle are known:

    DB_SSLMODE=verify-full
    DB_SSLROOTCERT=<path to that environment's real CA bundle file>

Do not invent or hardcode a CA path, hostname, or `.pem`/`.crt` file in
`database.py` or `env.py` -- an unverifiable guessed value is worse than an
explicit "not yet configured". If the production RDS CA bundle isn't
available in this repository/environment, leave `DB_SSLMODE`/
`DB_SSLROOTCERT` unset in production and treat it as a manual
operator/infra task (see also: `rds.force_ssl` or the equivalent
server-side enforcement setting, which is independent of and complements
this client-side setting -- neither one alone is sufficient).

Deployment sequence (ALEMBIC DEPLOYMENT ENFORCEMENT phase)
-------------------------------------------------------------
Two designs were compared for how a real deployment actually runs migrations
before starting the app:

  A. Container entrypoint -- bake `alembic upgrade head` into the Docker
     image's own boot sequence (an entrypoint script wrapping the existing
     `CMD`), so it runs automatically every time the container starts.
     Pro: impossible to forget. Con: it would re-run on EVERY container
     start, not just real deployments -- including a routine crash-recovery
     restart (e.g. Gunicorn's own worker respawn, a host reboot, `docker
     restart`) with no code/schema change involved at all. On a
     single-instance, single-worker deployment (workers=1, no rolling/
     blue-green replicas) that turns an ordinary restart into something that
     can fail outright if the DB is briefly unreachable at that exact
     moment, and it blurs "deploy new code+schema" together with "the
     process happened to restart" into the same event.

  B. Explicit deployment step -- a standalone script the deployer (CI/CD
     pipeline or an operator) runs BEFORE starting/restarting the app
     container, gating the app start on its exit code. Pro: a clear,
     auditable migration checkpoint, decoupled from routine process
     restarts; matches this project's actual release process (manual/
     CI-driven, not an orchestrator doing rolling replica swaps). Con: relies
     on the deployer actually running it -- there's no automatic enforcement
     baked into the container itself.

  SELECTED: Option B. This deployment has no rolling replicas for
  A's "impossible to forget" benefit to protect against multi-instance
  drift, and A's downside (coupling schema migration to every ordinary
  container restart) is a real, concrete risk for this single-worker setup.
  B gives the same auditable gate with none of that coupling.

Implementation: `backend/scripts/migrate_and_verify.py`. Invokes the real
`alembic` CLI as a subprocess (`alembic upgrade head`), then independently
verifies the database's post-upgrade `alembic current` matches `alembic
heads` computed from the migration files in the checkout actually running
(never a hardcoded revision string) -- so it never trusts Alembic's exit
code alone. Exits 0 only when both the upgrade succeeded AND the verified
revision check passes ("safe to start/restart the application"); exits 1
otherwise ("STOP -- do not start/restart"). It builds no database
connection or engine of its own -- all DB access happens through Alembic's
own `env.py` machinery (its own separate engine, `NullPool`), exactly like
running the `alembic` CLI by hand. Credentials are never printed; any
Alembic output containing a raw `postgresql://user:password@host` URL is
redacted before being surfaced. See `tests/test_migrate_and_verify.py` for
the full test coverage (successful migration, failure blocks deployment,
secrets never leak, head-verification catches a mismatch, missing
environment configuration).

The exact deployment sequence:

    python backend/scripts/migrate_and_verify.py && \
        docker compose up -d --no-deps app

(or the equivalent two steps in a CI/CD job, or run by hand by an operator)
-- if the script exits non-zero, the `&&` short-circuits and the app
container is never started/restarted with an unmigrated or
partially-migrated database underneath it.

This script is not wired into the Dockerfile `CMD`, `docker-compose.yml`, or
the CI workflow as of this phase -- it is a standalone tool the deployer
invokes as its own explicit step, per the Option B design above.
